--- spatially: is the failure cooperative? ---

  Share of a residue's chi1 neighbours within 10 A that are also in
  the wrong well. Stratified because burial predicts wrongness AND is
  spatially clumped, so the 'all' row cannot tell a cooperative
  failure from two surface residues happening to be neighbours. In
  the core and non-core rows both the subject and its neighbours are
  restricted to that class, which removes the confound.

  stratum     self wrong   self right   difference        n
  all              25.4%        20.0%        +5.5    17977
  core             18.6%        14.1%        +4.6     5741
  non-core         29.1%        24.1%        +5.1    12230

  base rate over everything :  22.7%

Reading it: a difference near zero in the *stratified* rows means
wrong wells are spatially independent and each residue fails alone.
A positive difference that survives stratification is a cooperative
failure -- an early wrong commitment propagating -- which is the
co-solving story and would make the clamp test's native arm the
place to look for the fix. The identity rule gives this statistic a
flat null, since its errors are independent uniform draws.

